If you are looking for racing, objectives or competition, please pass by.
If you want to feel what's it like to drive around total wilderness in huge, realistic, finely rendered all-terrain vehicles from the Soviet Era, please stay, you will love this game.
PROS:
- Enchanting attention to detail on vehicles and terrain;
- THE most realistic mud I have seen in a video game, which deforms and changes the quality of the surface as you drive over it again and again;
- Sound design is on point, the engine roars, the tires spin (pun fully intended) with characteristic rustling, the mud slurps, it's awesome;
- Driving at night is very immersive;
- EXTENSIVE modding support from community;
CONS:
- Lack of any kind of soundtrack, although I would say that it helps to be immersive like that - no music in the wilderness and you wouldn't hear it over the engine anyway;
- No real objectives to do, just simple "Unlock Garage" and "Deliver Logs from location A to location B", which repeat for every map;
VERDICT:
Very immersive experience, get the game to drive around, observe the scenery, admire the vehicles. If that's not enough, don't get the game.

I play the game heavily on iPad, and it is neat, but I have very few pros and quite a few contras to list as to why I am not recommending this game. If you are a free to play person and don’t want to invest money into something as short-lived as Fallout Shelter, you may have to buckle up because there will be big bumps down the road. I may repeat a few things, but everything is interconnected, so bear with me, please.
Let’s begin the review!
PROS:
- free to play – you are not forced to shell out hard-earned cash on the game in order to give it a try;
- fun, easy going, relaxing game which hilarious dwellers that say some cheesy stuff if you zoom in and read (especially flirting couples in living quarters);
- you can rename your dwellers all you like. My iPad Vault contains mash of FO and TES universes prominent characters so despite not having a legendary dweller by names of Paladin Danse and Arthur Maxson, I was able to "create" them via renaming tool and turn my Vault partly into Brotherhood of Steel HQ (given I have the themes for living quarters and diner to boot);
- quests are decent enough and early game are very captivating and addicting;
- very strong atmosphere and vibe of the main game.
CONTRAS:
- First of all, as I have mentioned above, if you are a F2P person, buckle up for… restarting you game entirely quite a few times until you hit you lucky stride right off the bet with guns/stimpacks/good legendary dwellers. I restarted my Vaults about 20 times or so until I got lucky with early lunchboxes from objectives and rolled the Vault from there.
- Inability to connect your iPad/iPhone game to the PC;
- Small starter budget. You start with 500 caps which is nothing, especially since Rushing heavily depends on Luck SPECIAL and none of your starter dweller will have it to ensure your room rushing is successful and one has no opportunity to level up dwellers’’ Luck SPECIAL early on since it takes a while before your Luck Training room becomes available;
- Every rush/dweller’s ability to find good stuff on quests and out in the Wastelands are entirely dependent on one SPECIAL trait – Luck. The rest make no real impact on the rooms dwellers are assigned to and such. It’s stupid that Diner requires Agility as Key SPECIAL, but to rush the room, Luck plays bigger part than aforementioned Agility.
- Complete and utter lack of weapons/stipmacks for early raiders/ghoul attacks. If early objective lunchboxes don’t drop them quickly, you are SoL and might as well restart the game anew;
- if you are an OCD builder like me, you will dislike one main thing about the game - inability to build full room, right off the bet pre-set location of elevators that one cannot move which in return off-sets the rest of the vault structure. Fully mergeable rooms are those that were built out of three blocks. However, the space you are given to work with prevents you from making every room in your vault built of three blocks to maximize the space and RSS gathering;
- inability to move your rooms/elevators around. Once you have built something the only way for you to move it is to demolish the room for skim compensation and build it elsewhere. However, you are not always able to do so due to rooms being connected to another rooms or elevators or w/e. Ergo, in some cases you are stuck with some of your building choices.
- Vault gets attacked by raiders/ghouls/deathclaws at the most inopportune moment. Sometimes its waves after waves after waves. Good luck if you have enough weapons to arm your dwellers, otherwise you are SoL. Radscorpions will demolish your dwellers if you don’t sit and watch the whole battle tapping in timely manner to heal/rad-cure your dwellers until the monster is dead. When deathclaws attack, dwellers health drop from merely presence of the DCs.
- Game constantly will yell at you for lack of storage. Upgrading medical bays/science labs will not increase storage capacity of the product they provide. You have to build more bays and labs to increase the cap for stimpacks and radaways. As a result, half of the vault would end up being medical facilities and warehouses.
